// Triage: a condition that compares a constant while an indexed read is also
// in scope.
//
// This generalizes ext/soap/php_sdl.c:304, where `ctx->sdl->source[4] == 's'`
// decides whether the URL is https:
//
//   *?{opcode: If || opcode: Loop} as $stmt
//   $stmt<cfgConditionValues> as $cond
//   $cond #> ?{opcode: param_member} as $indexed        // s[4], p[0], data[l-1]
//   $cond?{opcode: eq || ...} as $binDirect
//   $cond #> ?{opcode: eq || ...} as $binNested
//   $binDirect + $binNested as $bin
//   $bin #> ?{opcode: const} as $const
//
// What this proves: the branch condition compares against a constant, and an
// indexed read is reachable from the same condition.
//
// What this does NOT prove: that the constant comparison *is* the indexed
// read. `$bin` and `$indexed` are separate value sets, and SyntaxFlow has no
// "operand of" filter, so the two cannot be intersected in the rule. The
// exporter has to check the operand shape in the SSA text
// (`ParameterMember-...`); see common/syntaxflow/docs/sloppy-checks.md.
//
// The rule is deliberately silent about intent. Constants that are structural
// (NUL, newline, space) and files that are format parsers produce most of the
// hits; narrowing to "critical path" needs the project-specific context
// described in the same document.

*?{opcode: If || opcode: Loop} as $stmt
$stmt<cfgConditionValues> as $cond

$cond #> ?{opcode: param_member} as $indexed

$cond?{opcode: eq || opcode: neq || opcode: lt || opcode: gt || opcode: lteq || opcode: gteq} as $binDirect
$cond #> ?{opcode: eq || opcode: neq || opcode: lt || opcode: gt || opcode: lteq || opcode: gteq} as $binNested
$binDirect + $binNested as $bin

$bin #> ?{opcode: const} as $const